    {
      "title": "Verification-Aware Extreme-Event Bayesian Assay for the Completion Limit of the Periodic Table",
      "motivation": "The question of whether the periodic table will ever be complete now hinges on discovery verification as much as on nuclear synthesis, because IUPAC/IUPAP recognition requires sufficient evidence that a new atomic number has been produced and identified [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628). Hot-fusion surveys can propose projectile-target combinations for Z > 118, but predicted evaporation-residue cross sections are extremely small and strongly affected by quasifission, entrance-channel asymmetry, and survival probability [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3111464399). Theoretical predictions must also extrapolate nuclear interactions into poorly constrained superheavy regions, creating uncertainty in masses, fission barriers, and lifetimes [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4415525127). For the heaviest atoms, verification becomes a time-critical single-atom transport and chemistry problem, because recoils must be thermalized and delivered to detection before decay [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412961862). The primary testable hypothesis is that, when the candidate pool, surrogate models, and beam budget are held fixed, an acquisition policy that maximizes the end-to-end probability of an IUPAC-verifiable atom yields more verified atoms than a policy that maximizes production cross section alone. To test this, the idea transfers multi-fidelity Bayesian experimental design for extreme-event statistics from computational uncertainty quantification into superheavy-element discovery [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4213165830).",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the lack of a controlled, experimentally anchored way to decide whether elements beyond Z = 118 can be synthesized and verified before they decay; it can be divided into 4 parts: a fixed synthesis-and-stability candidate layer, a single-atom verification layer, an extreme-event multi-fidelity Bayesian acquisition layer, and a completion-boundary decision layer.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Fixed synthesis-and-stability candidate layer",
          "description": "Create a non-adapting candidate pool of projectile-target-energy combinations for Z = 119 to 126 using entrance-channel-dependent hot-fusion calculations that estimate compound-nucleus formation, survival probability, evaporation-residue cross sections, quasifission, and entrance-channel asymmetries [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3111464399). Attach uncertainty distributions to masses, fission barriers, half-lives, and decay chains by using superheavy-nucleus interaction models for the relevant mass region [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4415525127)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Single-atom verification layer",
          "description": "Convert each candidate into a probability of obtaining discovery-grade evidence by modeling recoil thermalization, gas-flow transport, ion transfer, and time losses with atmospheric-pressure ion-transfer simulations for a UniCell buffer-gas system [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412961862). Represent IUPAC/IUPAP atomic-number assignment as a probabilistic observation model that rewards correlated decay chains, chemical placement, or other element-specific evidence under low-count conditions [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Extreme-event multi-fidelity Bayesian acquisition layer",
          "description": "Borrow multi-fidelity Bayesian experimental design for extreme-event statistics from computational uncertainty quantification and use it to select the next reaction or beam energy [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4213165830). Treat cheap reaction, stability, and transport simulations as low-fidelity information and beam-time observations as high-fidelity information; the acquisition function maximizes the posterior probability of at least one verified atom, or the expected number of verified atoms, within a fixed beam-time budget."
        },
        {
          "name": "Completion-boundary decision layer",
          "description": "Use the posterior verified-event rate to classify each candidate element as physically inaccessible, verification-limited, or discoverable under a given facility. If the predicted verified-event rate remains below a discovery threshold even when verification efficiency is set to unity, the boundary is assigned to nuclear stability or production; if verification losses dominate, the boundary is assigned to capture, transport, or evidence collection, thereby giving an empirical way to address periodic-table completion [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628)."
        }
      ],
      "analogy_mapping": [
        {
          "inspiration": "Multi-Fidelity Bayesian Experimental Design to Quantify Extreme-Event Statistics",
          "concept": "Multi-fidelity Bayesian experimental design and acquisition for estimating extremely rare event probabilities",
          "adaptation": "Adapted to superheavy-element discovery by treating reaction synthesis, nuclear stability, gas transport, and single-atom verification as heterogeneous fidelity sources and selecting experiments that maximize the tail probability of at least one IUPAC-verifiable atom.",
          "citation": "[Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4213165830)"
        }
      ],
      "experiment_plan": [
        "Phase 1 setup: fix the candidate pool of Z = 119 and Z = 120 projectile-target-energy combinations, nuclear-stability priors, gas-cell geometry, transport times, detector efficiencies, decay data, and total beam-time budget so that only the selection policy can vary.",
        "Build a blind benchmark: calibrate surrogate models on historical or simulated outcomes from known superheavy isotopes, withhold a truth set of reaction-energy outcomes, and prevent every policy from seeing the truth set during selection.",
        "Run the full verification-aware extreme-event Bayesian policy and controls: production-only, transport-only, detection-only, fixed-weight unstructured mixture of normalized component scores, and random selection, with identical training data, candidate pool, compute budget, and number of proposed experiments.",
        "Primary one-factor test: vary only the acquisition objective/selection policy and keep all models, priors, and resources fixed; evaluate repeated trials on the blind truth set using expected verified atoms, posterior probability of at least one verified atom, and false-positive discovery rate.",
        "Exclude algorithmic confounders: verify surrogate calibration, repeat with equal numbers of low-fidelity evaluations, use leave-known-success-out splits, fix decay constants and chemical efficiencies, test prior sensitivity, and compare structured Bayesian acquisition with random selection and fixed-weight mixture to rule out chance and simple averaging.",
        "Attribute the effect to the claimed mechanism: ablate production, transport, and detection components alone; the full policy must outperform each single-component policy and the unstructured mixture, and improvement must appear when verification probability is included in the acquisition utility.",
        "Phase 2 physical validation: using a known short-lived recoil or homolog, compare method-selected conditions with production-only and random controls in randomized blocks while holding apparatus, gas cell, separator, detectors, beam dose, and target batch fixed; monitor target degradation, gas-flow drift, detector dead time, and background to exclude trivial explanations.",
