      "title": "Counterfactual Evidence-Accumulation Assay for the Verifiable Completion Boundary of the Periodic Table",
      "motivation": "The question of whether the periodic table can be completed is no longer only a synthesis question. IUPAC/IUPAP guidance emphasizes that element discovery depends on evolving verification methods and criteria [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628). Hot-fusion calculations identify possible projectile-target routes to Z > 118, but cross sections are tiny and quasifission and entrance-channel effects remain problematic [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3111464399). Predictions of stability and decay in this region depend on extrapolated nuclear interactions with limited experimental anchors [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4415525127), and chemical identification becomes a time-critical single-atom transport problem [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412961862). The unresolved bottleneck is therefore not a missing reaction list but the absence of a verification-aware decision framework that treats a discoverable element as an evidence-accumulation event and separates nuclear-stability limits from verification limits. The proposed method transfers hierarchical Bayesian evidence accumulation from atomic-physics data analysis [Author et al., 2017](https://openalex.org/W2558575588) and single-event residence-time analysis from single-molecule separation science [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3087606226) to superheavy-element discovery.",
