          "name": "Verification-information-gain acquisition policy",
          "description": "Define the next-action acquisition function as the expected reduction in entropy of the posterior over Z and isotope, or equivalently expected increase in log Bayes factor for an IUPAC-scale verification threshold, rather than expected produced or detected atoms [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628). The expected gain is computed over possible future decay-chain graphs and first-passage chemical observations. Actions include selecting a projectile-target combination, allocating beam dose, adjusting gas-flow/chemistry conditions, running calibration sources, or measuring background. The policy uses posterior predictive simulations from the digital twin and can choose calibration when drift or dead-time uncertainty makes calibration more informative than additional beam time, extending Bayesian atomic-physics experimental design to sequential single-atom superheavy verification [Author et al., 2017](https://openalex.org/W2558575588)."
        },
        {
          "name": "Counterfactual completion-boundary decomposition",
          "description": "For each candidate element/isotope, compute discovery probability under actual conditions and under explicit interventions: perfect production, perfect transport, perfect detection, and perfect calibration. Production counterfactuals are bounded by optimistic hot-fusion cross-section predictions [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3111464399); transport/detection counterfactuals idealize the gas-flow and detector stages [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412961862); stability uncertainty is propagated from superheavy nuclear-interaction models [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4415525127). If perfect verification does not make discovery probable, the candidate is stability/production-limited; if perfect verification does, it is verification-limited. This turns the qualitative question of periodic-table completion into a quantitatively decomposed boundary problem [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628)."
