      "motivation": "The question whether the periodic table can ever be complete is not only a synthesis problem; it is also a verification problem because IUPAC/IUPAP recognition depends on evidence that can become extremely difficult to obtain for few short-lived atoms [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3048546628). Hot-fusion reaction surveys identify plausible projectile-target routes toward elements beyond Z = 118 and predict potentially accessible evaporation-residue cross sections, but entrance-channel effects, quasifission, and survival probabilities remain major obstacles [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3111464399). Theoretical predictions for superheavy stability must extrapolate nuclear interactions into sparsely constrained regions, so lifetimes and fission barriers are uncertain [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4415525127). At the same time, chemical identification beyond the heaviest studied elements is a single-atom thermalization and transport challenge [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4412961862). The field therefore lacks a quantitative estimand that separates a physical nuclear-existence limit from a verification limit. This proposal imports single-entity residence-time analysis from single-molecule separation science [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3087606226) and uncertainty-quantified reaction-network inference from Bayesian scientific machine learning [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4392592391) to create a testable verification first-passage assay for superheavy-element discovery.",
